# SYNOPSIS

```ndb
# a comment
kind=name key=value flag text="a value with spaces" bytes=x"610a62"
    more=value                  # an indented line continues the record
```

# DESCRIPTION

Every structured text file in the system is ndb, the format of Plan 9's network database: the service and driver manifests (svc(6), driver(6)), the build's port files, the manual's page headers (guide(6)), the store's and the boot slots' records (store(6), release(6), slots(6)), the records of a spawn message, and what servers print in files such as `status` and `info`.
There is no TOML, JSON or YAML in the base system.

One parser and one writer, `lib/vx-ndb`, serve every program that reads or writes it: the kernel, svcd(8), devmgr(8), the servers, the C library's back end and `build` on the host.
No program parses ndb or formats it by hand, so a value that comes from another program, a file name or a window title, can never forge a tuple or a record.

ndb has no keys of its own.
It is the syntax; what each key means, which are required, and whether an unknown one is refused belongs to the format built on it, and that format's page lists them.
Unlike Plan 9's ndb, a key appears at most once in a record.

The text is UTF-8 (see utf(6)), in lines ended by a newline.
A space and a tab are the same everywhere.

: record
  One line of tuples, and the lines after it that start with a space or a tab, which continue it.
  A line that starts with neither starts the next record.
  Blank lines and comment lines may come between a record's lines without ending it.
  A record holds at most 128 tuples and 64 KiB.
: tuple
  `key=value`, or a bare `key`, which is a flag that is set.
  Tuples are separated by spaces and tabs; there may be any number of them on a line.
: key
  One or more bytes, none of them a space, a newline, `=`, `"` or a control character, that are valid UTF-8.
  By convention a record's first tuple says what kind of record it is (`service=`, `port=`, `page=`), and dots group related keys (`mem.free`).
: bare value
  The bytes after `=` up to the next space or newline: at least one, valid UTF-8, with no `"` and no control character.
  It may hold `=` and `#`.
: quoted value
  A `"`, then any valid UTF-8 but a control character, then a `"`, with `""` inside standing for one quote.
  It ends on the line it starts on.
  `""` is the empty value, which cannot be written bare.
: hex value
  `x"`, an even number of hexadecimal digits in either case, and `"`: the bytes the digits spell, whatever they are.
  It is how a value holding a newline, a tab, another control character or invalid UTF-8 is written: a file named `a`, newline, `b` is `name=x"610a62"`.
: comment
  A `#` where a tuple could start, and the rest of its line.
  Anywhere else `#` is an ordinary byte, so `key=a#b` has the value `a#b`.
: list
  Values separated by commas, in one value: `keys=formats,files`.
  The parser does not split it; the format that reads the key does.
: number
  Decimal digits with no leading zero, or `0x` and lower-case hexadecimal digits, with no sign and within 64 bits, as `vx_ndb_get_u64` reads a value.
  The writer writes numbers in decimal, and a negative one with a leading `-`, which a format reads itself.

There are no types and no nesting in the file.

The parser is strict.
A record with a duplicate key, bad quoting, a control character, invalid UTF-8, or too many tuples or bytes is refused whole, with the line of the fault, and never repaired, so two readers can never read one record differently.
A carriage return is a control character, so a file with CRLF line ends is refused.
The parser stops at the first error.

The writer chooses each value's form itself: bare when the value is not empty and holds no space, `"` or control character and is valid UTF-8; quoted when it is valid UTF-8 with no control character but is empty or holds a space or a `"`; hex otherwise.
A tab is a control character, so a value holding one is written in hex.
It writes a record as one line, its tuples separated by one space, and never writes continuation lines or comments.
It refuses a key it could not read back, one that is empty, starts with `#`, or holds a space, a newline, `=`, `"`, a control character or invalid UTF-8, and a record that does not fit the caller's buffer; a refused record is not used.

# EXAMPLES

A manifest, from `boot/svc/system.ndb`, with flags and two records:

```ndb
service=sys-fsd program=/boot/bin/fsd post=fsd console entropy pager
    when=vx.system
connect=disk0.system
```

A record continued on an indented line, and a quoted value: this page's header.

```ndb
page=ndb sect=6 summary="the one text format: records of key=value tuples"
    src=lib/vx-ndb/ndb.h,lib/vx-ndb/ndb.c
```

Hex values, from `tests/qemu/cons.ndb`, where the bytes typed include backspace, delete and carriage return:

```ndb
# "café", DEL, "e", return: the erase takes back é's two bytes (ADR-0013)
type=x"636166c3a97f650d"
expect="constest: got [cafe]"
```

# FILES

: `/boot/svc/*.ndb`
  Service manifests; see svc(6).
: `/boot/drv/*.ndb`
  Driver manifests; see driver(6).
: `ports/*/port.ndb`
  How `build` builds each vendored port, in the repository.

